# Tooling that runs *in* this repo but is not *part of* the bundle.
#
# .harness/ ships its own .gitignore for runtime artifacts, but never names the
# directory itself, so it sat permanently untracked in `git status` — noise that
# trains you to skim the one command that tells you what is uncommitted. It is
# also private-org tooling (see EXCLUDED.md) and has no business in a public
# bundle. Ignored here rather than in .harness/.gitignore because that file is
# tool-owned and regenerated.
#
# (.remember/ needs no entry — it self-ignores with a `*` in its own .gitignore.)
#
# AMENDED 2026-08-24 (spec D6, ADR-0001): the second reason above was factually
# wrong. `@harness-engineering/cli` resolves from npm without auth; only the
# ~50-command plugin layer is private-marketplace, and that stays in the company
# overlay. So the directory is no longer ignored wholesale — its own tool-owned
# .gitignore keeps the runtime artifacts out, which is what reason (a) about
# `git status` noise actually needed. The public/private split is recorded in
# docs/knowledge/decisions/0001-*.md rather than being re-litigated here;
# EXCLUDED.md rows 14-15 split in M3 (SC-9).
#
# The two rules below follow the shape already in use in the sibling repo that
# has tracked .harness/ the longest: track the ledgers, ignore the generated
# output and anything machine-identifying.

# Extraction output — generated, regenerated per machine, and zero-byte here
# today. Ignored as a whole directory rather than only knowledge/extracted/,
# because it is the tool's output root and its subdirectory layout is the
# tool's to change.
.harness/knowledge/

# Per-machine identity and telemetry. These are ALSO listed in the tool-owned
# .harness/.gitignore, and the duplication is deliberate: that file is
# regenerated by `harness upgrade`, so a protection living only there has a
# third-party owner who can drop it without a diff anyone reviews. Restated in
# a file this repo owns. This repo is public and exists to move a setup between
# laptops — a machine id reaching it is the leak, not the noise.
**/.harness/.install-id
**/.harness/telemetry.json
**/.harness/.telemetry-notice-shown

# Permission allowlists must never be inherited between machines. EXCLUDED.md
# has the long version: an allowlist is a config file nobody thinks to audit, so
# pasted payload data sits in it indefinitely. Grow it from real prompts on the
# machine you are on. This repo exists to move a setup between laptops, which is
# exactly the vector — so the ignore lives here, not only in a global excludes
# file that a fresh clone elsewhere would not have.
.claude/settings.local.json

# macOS
.DS_Store
